# Remote timeouts — fail fast, then make it fast

Prefer a **low** timeout and a **fast** command. Do not pad "just in case".
If a job needs minutes, remove the hot path or parallelize; do not raise
the clock.

`efs-ssh.sh` defaults to **15s** (`EFS_SSH_TIMEOUT`). SSH `ServerAlive`
does not kill D-state (`fusermount3`, FUSE `request_wait_answer`).

- Budget = expected runtime + small slack. Probe/kill 10, rsync+make 60,
  edelete 30, posix test 15, posix suite 180, posix2 step 15, perf 400.
- Parallelize independent hosts (wipe kills, setup, 9-way posix).
- Poll instead of `sleep 5` for mounts. Kill `efs-fuse` before
  `timeout 3 fusermount3`.
- Timeout is a **FAIL**, not a skip. Do not retry-until-forever.
- No `EFS_SSH_TIMEOUT=0`. Raise only after the work is already fast.
- **Anything estimated over 10 s does not run in the login-node shell.**
 Launch it in a detached GNU screen on **node9901, fstor007, or an
 fcstor**:
 `~/.cursor/skills/efs-test-ssh/scripts/efs-bg.sh start <name> '<cmd>'`
 (default runner `node9901.ib`; `EFS_RUNNER=fstor007.ib` or
 `EFS_RUNNER=fcstor007.ib`). The login node only issues that start
 (expected under 10 s) and then reads `~/efs-runs/<name>.log`.
 `efs-bg.sh status` reads the log locally. Do not `efs-bg.sh wait` —
 it sleeps on the login node. The Cursor shell there dies even on short
 cluster commands once the session is long ("no exit status", Sep 20–21)
 and the tool result then has no stdout, no stderr, and no exit code.

## "no exit status" — read the transcript, do not guess

That result means the tool channel died. It does not say whether the
command started, and it does not kill a screen already running on
node9901.

Every login-node Shell command goes through the recorder, including
`echo alive`, `efs-bg.sh start`, and a short ssh. One unique name per
attempt:

```
~/.cursor/skills/efs-test-ssh/scripts/efs-rec.sh <name> '<cmd>'
```

The transcript is `~/efs-runs/rec-<name>.log`. A start line is appended
before `<cmd>` runs; an end line carries `rc=`. The file is append-only.

**The channel can execute one tool call several times** (Sep 23 2026:
three `efs-rec: start` lines within one second for a single `Shell`
call — three concurrent builds in one `/tmp/efs`, three `git commit`s).
`efs-rec.sh` therefore runs a name once (`flock` on the log + a `.done`
marker; duplicates log `efs-rec: DUPLICATE` and exit 75) and
`efs-bg.sh start` holds a per-name lock. Never issue a mutating command
(roll, deploy, wipe, commit) outside `efs-rec.sh`, and never reuse a
name to retry — a new attempt is a new name.

When the tool returns "no exit status":

1. Stop issuing more Shell calls.
2. Read `~/efs-runs/rec-<name>.log` with the file tools. File reads still
   work after the shell channel dies.
3. If `<cmd>` was `efs-bg.sh start <screen>`, also read
   `~/efs-runs/<screen>.log` and `<screen>.rc`.
4. Say what the file shows:
   - no new `efs-rec: start` line — the wrapper never ran
   - start line, output, no `efs-rec: end` — it started; the channel died
     mid-run; the partial stdout/stderr in that file is the detail
   - `efs-rec: end … rc=N` — it finished; only the tool result was lost
   - screen log contains `efs-bg: start` and no `efs-bg: end` — that
     screen is running on node9901
   - screen log absent — `efs-bg.sh start` did not create it
5. Do not report "the command did not run" unless the transcript says
   the wrapper never started.
